  'brief-history',
  'A brief history of ਮੱਲੂ ਨੰਗਲ',
  'Every fact this archive can currently cite: place, name, the Guru Arjan tradition, census, land, school, and the gurdwara account.',
  $brief$ਮੱਲੂ ਨੰਗਲ (also spelled Mallu Nangal, and Malu Nangal in the Census of India) is a gram panchayat village in the Majha region of Amritsar district, Punjab. The PIN code is 143101. The postal head office is Raja Sansi. Directories place it in Ajnala tehsil and the Harsha Chhina (Harsa Chhina) block; some also name Lopoke tehsil. It falls in the Amritsar assembly constituency and the Amritsar Lok Sabha constituency, and is headed by an elected sarpanch. GeoNames lists the place at about 31.75° N, 74.85° E, roughly 230 metres above sea level.

Distances vary by the directory that prints them, so they are given as printed. One places the village about 13 kilometres north of Amritsar city and about 9 kilometres from Harsha Chhina. Another says about 18 kilometres from Ajnala and about 18 kilometres from Amritsar. Punjab Kosh says about 10 kilometres from Majitha. Raja Sansi is the nearest town, about 5 kilometres away. Neighbours named in directories are Adliwala and Loharka Kalan (about 2 kilometres), Ranewali (about 2 kilometres), Jagdev Kalan (about 3 kilometres), and Loharka Khurd (about 4 kilometres). An all-weather road and bus service are recorded. The nearest railway station named in one directory is Verka Junction. Sri Guru Ram Dass Jee International Airport is about 6 kilometres away by air. The same directory names the Ravi and the Kiran Saiki nala as waters in the wider area, not as streams inside the village.

How the village got its name is tradition, not a dated land record, and two published tellings disagree.

Punjab Kosh, volume 2, says the older name was Lille. Guru Arjan Dev Ji, travelling toward Guru Ke Bagh, stopped and asked for water. A servant drew it from a well in a clay pot. A Pathan of Lille broke the pot. The Guru is quoted: “ਜਾਣਗੇ ਲਿੱਲੇ, ਆਉਣਗੇ ਗਿੱਲੇ” — the Lille will go; the Gills will come. Mallu Gill, grazing buffaloes outside, gathered about twenty to twenty-five Sikhs that night. The Pathans left. In the morning the Guru named the village ਮੱਲੂ ਨੰਗਲ. The Kosh says most residents are of the Gill got.

A second account, filed from a gurdwara directory (HistoricalGurudwaras.com) and not re-opened page by page, says the older name was Kho Nangal and the people were Lile Pathans. Guru Arjan was travelling toward Guru Ka Bagh with Raja Hari Sen of Suket. Pathans damaged the water pitcher. Bhai Mallu Ji of Jagdev Kalan later destroyed that settlement and founded a new one called Malu Nangal, with the Guru’s blessings. That account does not show a writing in which the Guru ordered the attack, and it gives no year.

No year is assigned. No source found here places Guru Nanak, Guru Hargobind, Guru Tegh Bahadur, or Guru Gobind Singh in this village. Bhai Mallu of the sakhi is not Bhai Mallu Saahi of Guru Angad’s time. Nearby, but separate, Gurdwara Guru Ka Bagh at Ghukkevali in Ajnala tahsil is recorded as a stay of Guru Arjan in the Majha and as the place of the Raja Hari Sen sakhi; Guru Tegh Bahadur is associated with that garden in 1664. Those pages do not name ਮੱਲੂ ਨੰਗਲ.

Guru Arjan’s own life is documented apart from the village: born 1563, Guru from 1581 to 1606, Harmandir Sahib completed and the Adi Granth installed at Amritsar in 1604, death in Mughal custody at Lahore in 1606.

Punjab Kosh describes Gurdwara Mallu Nangal Sahib outside the village on the road from Amritsar toward Jagdev Kalan: a white-marble building, khandas on the domes, a Nishan Sahib of about 105 feet, a small Sikh-history library, twelve rooms for visitors, Gurpurabs, Sangrand gatherings, and langar, looked after by a local gurdwara committee. This archive does not yet hold a photograph, an inscription, or a foundation date for that building.

An editable public directory adds an unsourced note that an Ansari Muslim family and a mosque were here, and that the family left for Pakistan at Partition. That note can be changed by anyone. It is recorded only as a lead, not as established history.

The earliest population figure in these sources is 1,752 in 1981, printed in Punjab Kosh.

The Census of India 2011 (village code 037396) counted 2,552 people in 456 households: 1,349 males and 1,203 females. Children aged 0–6 numbered 348 (201 boys and 147 girls). Scheduled Caste population was 747 (376 males and 371 females). Scheduled Tribe population was 0. Literate people numbered 1,493 (838 males and 655 females); illiterate people numbered 1,059. Directory summaries give the literacy rate as 58.5 percent. The village area was 428 hectares. Of that, 364 hectares were sown and irrigated by tube wells, and 64 hectares were non-agricultural. Wheat, rice, and maize are the crops named. One directory’s note of eight hours of farm power in summer and ten in winter should be checked against the electricity record before it is treated as current.

A government primary school, GES / GPS Mallu Nangal (UDISE 03020300601), in Harsha Chhina block, is recorded as opening in 1925. It is co-educational, classes 1 to 5, under the Department of Education. Census notes also mention government pre-primary, middle, and secondary schooling in the village. A government high school, GHS Mallu Nangal, is listed, and a private primary school, Sri Guru Arjun Dev Model School. The “Mallu Nangal” school cluster covers about twenty-one schools in the surrounding villages; those are not all inside this village.

What is still missing, and is not invented here: the year the village was founded, which of the two name-stories is older, Mallu’s father’s name and lineage, a photograph of the gurdwara, family papers, and any hukamnama or manuscript that actually belongs to ਮੱਲੂ ਨੰਗਲ. Those belong on the Contribute page, with a date and a source.$brief$,
  'Brief history',
  0,
  'Recorded facts to 2011',
  '/images/mustard-fields.jpg',
  'Census of India 2011, village Malu Nangal (code 037396). Punjab Kosh, vol. 2, via Punjabi Pedia (Ajit, 7 August 1992; 1981 Amritsar handbook). UDISE / school directories for GPS and GHS Mallu Nangal. Public village directories for PIN 143101, neighbours, tehsil, airport, and the unverified Partition note. Encyclopaedia of Sikhism and Discover Sikhism for Gurdwara Guru Ka Bagh, Ghukkevali — a different village. HistoricalGurudwaras.com account of this village as supplied to the archive on 14 October 2026.',
  'Archive editor',
  '2026-10-14',
  '2026-10-14',
  false
)
